  Only the primary weapon matters for calculating to-hit.  If it hits,
the secondary weapon automatically hits.

"* If you are using two-weapon combat, then if your primary weapon hits
and is not destroyed in the process, and the monster is not dislodged
from its position, your secondary weapon also automatically hits."

  Two more differences between single weaponing and twoweaponing.  They
use different skill tables for calculating damage.

               NEEDED        WEAPON       2-WEAP   
SKILL LEVEL   HITS  EXP    +HIT +DAM    +HIT +DAM  
~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~  ~~~    ~~~~ ~~~~    ~~~~ ~~~~  
Unskilled       0    0      -4   -2      -9   -3   
Basic          20    1       0    0      -7   -1   
Skilled        80    2      +2   +1      -5    0   
Expert        180    3      +3   +2      -3   +1   


  The twoweapon table is much worse than the single weapon table.


  The lower of #twoweapon and weapon skill are used.  E.g. if unskilled
in twoweapon but Expert with your primary weapon your primary weapon
will be treated as Unskilled while #twoweaponing.  This makes it
difficult to work up from Unskilled in #twoweapon.

  This means that when first #twoweaponing both weapons do damage as if
unskilled.  That's -3 per weapon, for -6 total.  Add in the -9 to hit
and Unskilled twoweaponing looks like a good way to get killed.
(Especially as melee fighting is the best way to die in NetHack.  If the
monster's in range, so are you.)


